# Every status bit, and the reserved spans.
#
# HAND BUILT FROM THE SPECIFICATION. Every status word in the capture is 010Fh
# — the same five bits, on every one of its 857 lines — so seven of Table 4's
# twelve bits had never been rendered by anything, and neither had the reserved
# warning.
#
# The bits are all one code path, so this is not about finding a decode bug. It
# is about the names: a bit whose name has never been printed is a name nobody
# has ever read out of this decoder, and Figure 16's stored description gets
# four of these positions wrong (it puts the flash bits at 15-14 and 13-12).
#
# --- 1. Every defined bit set, no reserved bits ---
#
#   25     GET_STATUS
#   FB     CRC
#
#   08     ACCEPT
#   FF 33  Status 33FFh, least significant byte first (section 5.1, p.86)
#   1F     CRC
#
# 33FFh is bits 0-9 and 12-13: the twelve bits Table 4 pp.31-33 defines, and no
# others. Bits 11:10 and 15:14 are Reserved and stay clear here.
#
# CRC bytes pasted from tools/crc_ref.py (rule R4).
CMD 25 FB
TAR
RSP 08 FF 33 1F

# --- 2. Every bit set, reserved spans included ---
#
# FFFFh, so the two reserved spans carry CC00h between them. §6.2 requires them
# to be driven to zero, so this is the target misbehaving and the decoder says
# so — the only fixture in the suite that reaches that path.
#
# The reserved mask is the reason this matters: read from Figure 16's stored
# description the flash bits sit at 15:12 and the mask would be F000h. Read off
# the page they sit at 8, 9, 12 and 13, so it is CC00h. Only a word with those
# bits set can tell the two apart in a decode.
CMD 25 FB
TAR
RSP 08 FF FF 75
